<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="fo8" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>FO8: Bifurcations</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can determine the bifurcation values of a one-parameter family of differential equations and describe the corresponding change in solution behavior using phase lines.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-bifurcations">
        <title>Bifurcations</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-one-parameter-family">
            <title>One-Parameter Family</title>
            <idx><h>differential equation (one-parameter family)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   A <term>one-parameter family</term> of differential equations is a collection which depends on some parameter, often denoted <m>\lambda</m> or <m>\mu</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the equation
                    <md>
                       \dfrac{dx}{dt} = x^2 - 10x + 24
                    </md>
                    It can (and should) be checked that there are two equilibrium solutions, one at <m>x = 4</m> and one at <m>x = 6</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The phase diagram for the differential equation shows that that <m>x = 4</m> is a sink while <m>x= 6</m> is a source. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    If we make the constant term a parameter, we have
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{dx}{dt} = x^2 - 10x + \mu
                    </md>
                    Now the phase diagram depends on the value of <m>\mu</m>; if <m>\mu = 25</m>, for example, we have one equilibrium solution at <m>x = 5</m>, while there are no equilibrium solutions for <m>\mu \gt 25</m>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <definition xml:id="def-bifurcation">
            <title>Bifurcation</title>
            <idx><h>bifurcation</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <term>bifurcation</term> in the solution to a one-parameter family of differential equations is a change in the long-term behavior of the solution.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For the equation
                    <md>
                        x' = x^2 - 10x + \mu
                    </md>
                    There is a bifurcation at <m>\mu = 25</m> because for values <m>\mu \lt 25</m>, there are two equilibrium solutions, one at <m>\mu = 25</m>, and none for <m>\mu \gt 25</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The equation 
                    <md>
                        y' = y^3 - \mu y
                    </md>
                    has equilibrium points at <m>y = 0</m> and <m>y = \pm \sqrt{\mu}</m>. Because the latter equilibria don't exist for <m>\mu \lt 0</m>, there is a bifurcation at <m>\mu = 0</m>.                   
                </p>
                <p>
                    We can capture this behavior using phase lines and a phase diagram.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fo8-phasediagram-1">
                    <image width="60%">
                        <xi:include href="./img/fo8-phaseline-1.xml" />
                    </image>
                    <caption>The phase diagram for <m>y' = y^3-\mu y</m></caption>
                </figure>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>
    
</section>